Commentary of al-Biqa'i (Nazm al-Durar) on surah 67, verse 13:

When fear (khashyah) is among the inward acts, and everyone claims that he fears God, He said, warning them with His knowledge and calling them to watch over Him (muraqabah), lest they be deceived by His forbearance, linking it to what is implied as the reason for making muraqabah obligatory: they conceal their deeds and display them. ﴿And keep your speech secret, or declare it aloud﴾, that is, O creatures.

Since the singular noun for a kind (al-jins) indicates both its little and its much, He said: ﴿your speech﴾ (p-243), that is, whether it is good or bad, ﴿or declare it aloud﴾, for He knows it and will recompense you for it, because His knowledge does not need a cause. This is because the polytheists used to say: "Keep your speech secret, lest the god of Muhammad hear." Then He gave the reason for that, emphasizing it because of people's remoteness from this idea, with His words: ﴿Indeed, He﴾, that is, your Lord, ﴿is All-Knowing﴾, that is, of perfect knowledge, ﴿of what is within the breasts﴾, that is, of their reality, their essence, their state, their natural disposition, and what arises from them, whether the breast has imagined it without expressing it, or it is something it has not yet imagined, as is evident from what He, glorified and exalted, reports about them of what occurred while they were concealing it, or what has not yet occurred and then occurs, as He, glorified, has informed;
